    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rosanky, Texas takes its name from Ed Rosanky, a Prussian immigrant who settled in the area in 1854.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/rosanky-tx",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "When the railroad arrived in the early 1890s, Ed Rosanky donated land for a station and built a general store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/rosanky-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before becoming Rosanky, the community was known as Snake Prairie, with a school opened in 1868 and a post office in 1871.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/rosanky-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town grew largely from German settlers who used it as a trading hub for surrounding livestock‑raising operations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
